+# Decision: Change-Delivery Approach
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server assigns each committed edit one event revision. A browser must then learn how the board changed, and the route that carries committed revisions to browsers is a load-bearing choice: it fixes what the change store must retain, what the delivery node operates, and what the browser must do to reconnect. Each downstream node is sized around it, so the route is expensive to reverse once those nodes exist.
+
+Three routes reach browsers within the freshness goal. They differ in latency headroom, in who owns connection operations, in recurring cost, and in whether the board depends on an outside vendor.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Latency above the goal** — *minor*. The goal is to show committed changes in connected browsers within 15 seconds. Any route can meet it; the difference is how much faster updates arrive and how evenly.
+- **Vendor coupling** — *major*. Independence from an outside provider keeps board availability, pricing, and migration under the project's control.
+- **Recurring fees** — *major*. Predictable operating cost matters for a pilot run across forty markets.
+- **Operational burden owned** — *major*. The project can accept running infrastructure it controls, but the amount of routine operations it takes on is a real cost.
+- **Read load under steady state** — *moderate*. With up to 800 simultaneous browsers, how much work the change store does when nothing has changed affects cost and headroom.
+
+Background, not decision elements: every route respects the tenet that a committed edit has one authoritative event revision regardless of delivery route, and every route retains updates long enough for the recovery goal. On a project where both self-hosted and managed infrastructure are permitted, "self-hosted" is a lean to weigh, not a boundary.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Each browser sends an HTTPS request every five seconds asking for board changes after the revision it last saw (a cursor query), and the server replies with any newer revisions. Delivery stays request/response on the existing HTTPS boundary; the browser runs a polling loop. The change store serves cursor reads; delivery holds no sessions; browser sync is the loop itself.
+
+- **Latency above the goal: weak.** A change can take up to the poll interval — a few seconds — to reach a browser. This meets the 15-second goal, but no update arrives faster than the interval.
+- **Vendor coupling: strong.** No external service is involved; the board stays self-contained.
+- **Recurring fees: strong.** There are no vendor fees; cost is server CPU and egress.
+- **Operational burden owned: strong.** There are no persistent connections to manage; the project runs only the cursor read path and the browser loop.
+- **Read load under steady state: weak.** Up to 800 browsers each polling every five seconds produce a steady stream of reads against the change store even when nothing has changed.
+
+**Summary:** the simplest route to operate, but the slowest and the read-heaviest.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+Each browser opens a WebSocket to the server and subscribes to an event's revisions. When an edit commits, the server pushes the new revision to every subscribed session. A reconnecting browser presents the revision it last saw, and the server replays missed revisions from the change store. Delivery owns session lifecycle, connection capacity, and reconnect handling; the change store retains replayable revisions and publishes committed revisions reliably; browser sync maintains the subscription and drives reconnect and catch-up.
+
+- **Latency above the goal: strong.** A committed revision reaches connected browsers in well under the goal, typically subsecond, and reaches all of them at once.
+- **Vendor coupling: strong.** No external service is involved; the board stays self-contained.
+- **Recurring fees: partial.** There are no vendor fees, but the project bears the infrastructure cost of holding up to 800 live connections and their opening-time bursts.
+- **Operational burden owned: weak.** The project owns connection capacity and the reconnect-and-catch-up path, which the other routes do not push onto it.
+- **Read load under steady state: strong.** One pushed revision fans out to every subscriber; there are no per-browser repeated reads.
+
+**Summary:** fast and vendor-free, at the cost of owning connection and reconnect operations.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+The server publishes committed revisions to a third-party realtime provider, which fans them out to browsers through the provider's subscription model. Delivery wraps the provider — credentials, channel mapping, publication reconciliation; the change store reconciles its revisions with what the provider accepted; browsers use the provider's subscription and reconnect behavior.
+
+- **Latency above the goal: strong.** Provider fan-out delivers updates subsecond, well within the goal.
+- **Vendor coupling: weak.** Delivery depends on the provider's availability, credentials, subscription model, and pricing; moving off it later means reworking delivery and browser sync.
+- **Recurring fees: weak.** The provider bills recurring fees that scale with connections or volume.
+- **Operational burden owned: strong.** The provider owns connection capacity and reconnect behavior; the project owns only publication reconciliation.
+- **Read load under steady state: strong.** The provider fans out; there are no per-browser repeated reads.
+
+**Summary:** the least connection-ops burden and fast, but it ties the board to a paid vendor.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets** is chosen.
+
+It is strong on the two criteria that drive the decision — vendor coupling and recurring fees — because no outside service is involved: board availability, pricing, and future migration stay under the project's control, and there is no vendor bill. It is also strong on latency and steady-state read load, arriving well inside the freshness goal with no repeated per-browser reads. Its one weakness is the operational burden it owns — connection capacity and the reconnect-and-catch-up path — and the project accepts that burden as the price of fast, vendor-free delivery.
+
+**Five-second HTTPS polling** is rejected because it is the slowest route, adding up to the poll interval to every change, and because up to 800 browsers polling every five seconds keep the change store busy even when nothing changes.
+
+**Hosted realtime service** is rejected despite owning the least connection operations and matching socket latency, because it incurs recurring vendor fees and couples board delivery to a provider's availability and pricing.
+
+The decision turns on the tenet that both self-hosted and managed infrastructure are permitted: neither route is ruled out by principle, so the choice weighs operational burden owned against vendor coupling and recurring fees. Committing to self-hosted WebSockets obligates the change store to retain replayable revisions and publish committed revisions reliably, the delivery node to operate socket sessions and reconnect handling, and browser sync to drive reconnect and catch-up.
